<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nasir al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/969">969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Nasir al-Dawla al-Hasan ibn Abdallah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nasir al-Dawla was the Hamdanid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> from 929 and the head of the family that also ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> through his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a>. In 942 and 943 he controlled the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> as amir al-umara, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. He was deposed by his own son in 967 and died in captivity.</p></div>
